add(1996,7,4,'A composite number has a proper divisor at least its square root, bounding every composite candidate by k²; infinitude then forces k=1.','An infinite family with a fixed sum of proper divisors',r'Let $\sigma(n)$ be the sum of the positive divisors of $n$. Suppose a fixed positive integer $k$ and a strictly increasing infinite sequence $n_1,n_2,\ldots$ satisfy $\sigma(n_i)-n_i=k$. Prove that every $n_i$ is prime.',r'If $n$ is composite, its smallest prime factor $p$ satisfies $p\le\sqrt n$. Thus $n/p$ is a proper divisor at least $\sqrt n$, giving $k=\sigma(n)-n\ge\sqrt n$ and $n\le k^2$. Only finitely many composite terms are therefore possible. Since the sequence is infinite, it contains a prime term, for which the sum of proper divisors is 1. Hence $k=1$. Every composite integer has at least the two proper divisors 1 and a prime factor, so its proper-divisor sum exceeds 1; also $\sigma(1)-1=0$. Consequently every term is prime.','Number Theory',['Divisors','Primes','Extremal bounds'])
add(1996,8,3.5,'A forbidden shift is a difference of two of the 45 occupied residue classes; 45·44+1<1996 leaves an allowed shift.')
