add(1997,4,5,'The infinite construction needs a dichotomy: infinitely many almost-monochromatic rows or columns, or a quadrant where both colours recur on every line.','An infinite monochromatic staircase',r'Colour each lattice point $(x,y)$ with nonnegative integer coordinates red or blue. Prove that there is an infinite monochromatic sequence $(x_1,y_1),(x_1,y_2),(x_2,y_2),(x_2,y_3),\ldots$, with both coordinate sequences strictly increasing.',r'If infinitely many horizontal lines have only finitely many blue points, select their heights increasingly. On each selected line all sufficiently far-right points are red; choose each next vertical segment beyond the thresholds for its two endpoint rows. This builds a red staircase. The same argument works after swapping colours or coordinates. Otherwise, each of these four exceptional-line families is finite. Beyond some coordinate threshold, every horizontal and vertical line contains infinitely many points of each colour. Begin at any point in that quadrant. Move upward to a point of the same colour, then rightward to another, and repeat. Each step can be made strictly positive and remains in the quadrant.','Combinatorics',['Colouring','Infinite constructions','Extremal arguments'],source=s)
add(1997,5,4,'The product-of-divisors formula turns equality into proportional prime-exponent vectors; the divisor counts then force the opposite ordering unless the vectors coincide.','The product of divisors determines the integer',r'Write $P(n)$ for the product of all positive divisors of $n$. Prove that positive integers $a,b$ with $P(a)=P(b)$ must satisfy $a=b$.',r'Let $d(n)$ be the number of divisors. Pairing complementary divisors gives $P(n)^2=n^{d(n)}$. Thus $a^{d(a)}=b^{d(b)}$. If either integer is 1, both are. Otherwise they have the same prime factors; write $a=\prod p_i^{\alpha_i}$ and $b=\prod p_i^{\beta_i}$. Equality implies $\alpha_i d(a)=\beta_i d(b)$ for every $i$. If $d(a)>d(b)$, then every $\alpha_i<\beta_i$, so $d(a)=\prod(\alpha_i+1)<\prod(\beta_i+1)=d(b)$, a contradiction. The reverse inequality is equally impossible. Therefore the divisor counts and all prime exponents agree.','Number Theory',['Arithmetic functions','Divisor product','Prime exponents'],source=s)
add(1997,6,3,'Squaring traps the sum of square roots between √(4n+1) and √(4n+3); no integer square lies between these endpoints.',source=s)
