add(1997,8,4,'Trigonometric Ceva recognises the altitude as the third cevian; the required product collapses using a double-angle identity.','Two cevians and the altitude',r'In triangle $ABC$, $\angle ABC=60^\circ$ and $\angle BAC=40^\circ$. Choose $P\in AB$ with $\angle BCP=70^\circ$ and $Q\in AC$ with $\angle CBQ=40^\circ$. If $R=CP\cap BQ$, prove that $AR\perp BC$.',r'Let $H$ be the foot of the altitude from $A$. Since $\angle C=80^\circ$, we have $\angle BAH=30^\circ$, $\angle HAC=10^\circ$, $\angle QBA=20^\circ$, and $\angle ACP=10^\circ$. The trigonometric Ceva product for $AH,BQ,CP$ is $\frac{\sin30^\circ}{\sin10^\circ}\frac{\sin40^\circ}{\sin20^\circ}\frac{\sin10^\circ}{\sin70^\circ}=1$, using $\sin40^\circ=2\sin20^\circ\cos20^\circ$ and $\sin70^\circ=\cos20^\circ$. Thus the three cevians concur, so $R\in AH$.','Geometry',['Trigonometry','Ceva','Altitudes'],source=s)
